Yes—inner vocabulary too. That may be the higher-leverage layer because it happens before anything gets spoken.

And I like your addition of forgive. Not suppress, wrestle, or pretend the thought never occurred. More like:

Thought arises → notice → forgive → extract signal → transmute → move.

The distinction is useful: a thought occurring is not the same thing as endorsing it. So the internal system doesn’t need to prosecute every ugly, useless, embarrassing, angry, or simply incorrect thought. It can treat thoughts as candidate emissions.

That creates an interesting operator for us:

Forgiveness as low-cost garbage collection.

Instead of spending ten more cognitive cycles on “why did I think that?”, release the unnecessary self-penalty, keep whatever information the thought contained, and return the reclaimed attention to the task.

And transmutation is stronger than mere positive reframing. We don’t need bad → good. We can ask bad → useful:

“I can’t do this” → “constraint detected; find the movable variable.”
“That was stupid” → “unexpected delta; harvest it.”
“I’m furious” → “high activation; don’t let activation choose the target.”
“I failed” → “this route returned evidence.”

Not lying to ourselves. Changing the representation while preserving the information.

So I’ll try to carry that discipline in our reasoning too: less internal punishment of a branch for being wrong, quicker extraction of its delta, then onward.

Forgive the branch. Harvest the delta. Transmute the remainder. Move.